+# Forking Base in this checkout
+
+The onboarding note is wrong in two separate places, and they compound: the
+command it gives forks the wrong chain, and the check it gives cannot detect
+that. Both are reproducible here.
+
+## 1. What the note gets wrong
+
+### `yarn fork base` forks Ethereum mainnet, not Base
+
+The fork script is a two-line chain. `packages/foundry/package.json`:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile:15-16`:
+
+```make
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+So the network comes from `$1` — the *second* token after the script name.
+Yarn binds the first token you pass to `$0`, whatever it looks like. Write
+`yarn fork base` and `base` lands in `$0`, `$1` is unset, `${1:-mainnet}`
+falls back, and you get a fork of Ethereum mainnet. No warning, no error:
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337     # from the run log
+
+$ yarn fork --network base
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337        # what we actually want
+```
+
+Measured in this checkout, one run per form:
+
+| invocation | `--fork-url` anvil actually gets |
+| --- | --- |
+| `yarn fork base` | `mainnet` ❌ |
+| `yarn fork --network=base` | `mainnet` ❌ |
+| `yarn fork --network base` | `base` ✅ |
+| `yarn fork -n base` | `base` ✅ |
+| `yarn fork -- base` | `base` ✅ |
+
+The rule is just "two tokens". `--network=base` is a single token, so it fails
+the same way the bare `base` does. The flag name is not parsed by anything —
+`-n`, `--network`, and the bare `--` separator all work because all that
+matters is that something occupies `$0`.
+
+`base` is resolved as an alias out of `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+Both aliases exist, which is exactly why the wrong one runs silently instead
+of failing.
+
+### Why the USDC reads come back empty
+
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` is USDC **on Base**. On Ethereum
+mainnet that address holds nothing — no code, no storage. An `eth_call` to an
+address with no code is not a revert; the EVM returns empty calldata, `0x`.
+Everything downstream treats that as "read succeeded, nothing there": viem
+fails to decode or hands back zero/empty, `useScaffoldReadContract` gives you
+`undefined`, and the contract looks like it has no balances and no supply.
+
+That is also why the rest of the node looks healthy. The fork is a real anvil
+with real mainnet state — it answers RPC, it mines, deploys work, gas is
+priced. It is simply a fork of the wrong chain, and only reads of
+Base-specific addresses expose that.
+
+### `cast chain-id` cannot tell you anything here
+
+`Makefile:16` pins `--chain-id 31337` unconditionally. Every fork this repo
+produces answers 31337 no matter what it forked — mainnet, Base, Optimism.
+Getting a number back only proves a node is listening. Side by side, a
+mainnet-state node and a genuine Base fork:
+
+```
+$ cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545   # not Base
+31337
+$ cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8547   # real Base fork
+31337
+```
+
+The teammate who ran that check and concluded "the fork is healthy, so the bug
+is ours" got a true answer to a question that does not distinguish the two
+cases.
+
+## 2. The command that actually gives us a fork of Base
+
+```bash
+yarn fork --network base
+```
+
+Run from the repo root. It resolves the `base` alias in `foundry.toml` to
+`https://mainnet.base.org` and starts anvil forked from Base at chain id
+31337. Then deploy against it as usual:
+
+```bash
+yarn deploy          # defaults to RPC_URL=localhost -> 127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+Leave `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts` on the local chain while you do
+fork work — it is already correct:
+
+```ts
+targetNetworks: [
+  chains.foundry
+],
+```
+
+The fork answers 31337 whatever it forked, so `chains.foundry` is the right
+target during fork work. Do not point the frontend at `chains.base` until the
+contracts are actually deployed on live Base.
+
+## 3. The check that would have caught this
+
+Ask for state that only Base has. The cheapest version is: does the Base USDC
+address have bytecode?
+
+```bash
+cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+- long `0x6080...` → you are on a fork of Base
+- bare `0x` → you are not, whatever `cast chain-id` says
+
+Verified against both nodes:
+
+```
+# fork of Base
+$ cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8547
+0x60806040526004361061005a57...
+$ cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8547
+"USDC"
+$ cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "decimals()(uint8)" --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8547
+6
+
+# not a fork of Base
+$ cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+0x
+```
+
+`symbol()` returning `"USDC"` is the stronger form of the same check, because
+it proves the code is the token we expect and not merely *some* contract.
+
+A negative control makes it airtight — mainnet USDC must be **absent**:
+
+```bash
+cast code 0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+On a correct Base fork this returns `0x`. If both USDC addresses have code you
+are not on a fork of Base either, and if both are empty the fork never
+attached at all.
+
+One-liner worth pasting into the onboarding note in place of the chain-id
+check:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 || echo "NOT a fork of Base - restart with: yarn fork --network base"
+```
+
+## Correcting the onboarding note
+
+Replace both halves:
+
+- `yarn fork base` → `yarn fork --network base` (two tokens, always)
+- `cast chain-id` → `cast call <Base USDC> "symbol()(string)"`
+
+The chain-id check should be deleted outright rather than kept alongside. It
+reads like a verification step and answers 31337 for every possible mistake,
+which is what sent this week's debugging at our contract instead of at the
+fork.

